<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tibetans</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tibetans are a people native to the Tibetan Plateau, speaking Tibetic languages and traditionally following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> and the Bon religion. United by the Yarlung kings in the 7th century, they built the Tibetan Empire that rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, and later lived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a> government. Today they live mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> Autonomous Region and neighbouring provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tibetans.jpg" alt="Tibetans" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arian Zwegers from Brussels, Belgium · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhongdian_festival_(6169776821).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tibetan tradition holds that the Tibetan people descend from the monkey Pha Trelgen Changchup Sempa and the rock ogress Ma Drag Sinmo. Genetic studies trace their roots to a deep East Asian lineage representing the indigenous population of the Tibetan plateau from about 40,000 to 30,000 years ago, joined within the last 10,000 years by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farmers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, who are associated with the spread of the Sino-Tibetan languages. Modern Tibetans are thought to derive up to 20 per cent of their ancestry from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> plateau population and most of the rest from Yellow River farmers. Most Tibeto-Burman speakers of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> are thought to descend from the ancient Qiang people.</p><p>Tibetans carry more than 30 genetic factors suited to high altitude, among them a variant of the EPAS1 gene, which regulates the body's production of haemoglobin. Researchers describe its spread over the past 5,000 years as possibly one of the fastest genetic changes observed in humans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most Tibetans practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, and a significant minority follow the indigenous Bon religion. According to legend the 28th king, Thothori Nyantsen, dreamed of a sacred treasure falling from heaven, holding a Buddhist sutra, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> did not take root until the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, who married two Buddhist princesses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhrikuti">Bhrikuti</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and Wencheng of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It gained popularity when Padmasambhāva visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> at the invitation of the 38th king, Trisong Deutson. Tibetan Buddhists walk clockwise around stupas and mani stones and chant Om mani padme hum, while followers of Bon walk in the opposite direction.</p><p>Tibetan art is deeply religious. Thangka paintings, a blend of Indian scroll painting with Nepalese and Kashmiri painting, appeared around the 8th century. Lhamo, the folk opera of dances, chants and songs, is traditionally said to have been founded in the 14th century by Thang Tong Gyalpo, a lama and bridge-builder. Traditional medicine draws on up to two thousand plants, and the physician Yuthog Yontan Gonpo of the 8th century is credited with the Four Medical Tantras. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a>, once the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, is regarded as the most important example of Tibetan architecture.</p><p>The 2010 Chinese census counted 6,282,187 Tibetans, of whom 2,716,388 lived in the Tibet Autonomous Region, with large numbers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, Qinghai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. Communities live also in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>, and nearly 100,000 Tibetans have lived in exile in India since 1959, many of them at Dharamshala and Bylakuppe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tibetans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Outside the nobility and the monasteries <b>Tibetans</b> were classed as serfs of two kinds: the düchung, bound to corvée labour on estates but holding their own plots and free to move outside their labour periods, and the village tralpa, bound for taxes and transport duties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Later <b>Tibetans</b> remembered Songtsen Gampo, Trisong Detsen and Ralpachen as the three Dharma kings, and the memory of the empire underpinned the legitimacy of later Tibetan rulers and lineages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 790 a Uyghur and Tang force was beaten by the <b>Tibetans</b> at Tingzhou (Beshbalik), but in 791 the Uyghurs drove the Tibetans from Lingzhou and defeated them and the Karluks at Beiting, sending the captured general Zan Rgyal Sum to Emperor Dezong.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tanguts, Dangxiang to the Chinese and Minyak to the <b>Tibetans</b>, came from the highlands of the Qinghai-Sichuan-Tibet region and were held to descend from the Western Qiang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Buddhist histories cast Bon in a hostile light, and <b>Tibetans</b> still call Buddhists nangpa, “insiders”, and Bonpos sometimes chipa, “outsiders”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Qing fell, <b>Tibetans</b> expelled the Chinese garrison in 1912, and the same government continued as Tibet (1912-1951).</blockquote><a href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a></li></ul></section>
